          <!-- Section: Ben Duoc Memorial -->
          <section id="ben-duoc-memorial" class="scroll-mt-28 space-y-5">
            <div class="border-l-4 border-amber-500 pl-4 mb-4">
              <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl lg:text-[26px] font-bold text-slate-900 leading-tight">
                Ben Duoc Memorial Complex & Heritage Setting
              </h2>
            </div>

            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px] leading-relaxed">
              Ben Duoc offers a distinctly different pace from the compact tunnel-only route. The memorial temple and landscaped grounds provide a quieter, more reflective part of the visit, contrasting with the physical tunnel experience.
            </p>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px] leading-relaxed">
              Ben Duoc Memorial Temple and its nine-storey memorial tower commemorate those who died in the Cu Chi area and during the revolutionary struggle. Beyond the temple, the complex encompasses:
            </p>

            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1.5 text-slate-700 text-[15px]">
              <li>Memorial sanctuary and landscaped gardens</li>
              <li>Nine-storey memorial observation tower</li>
              <li>Reconstructed wartime liberated zone displays</li>
              <li>Outdoor park and picnic groves</li>
              <li>Countryside refreshments and dining</li>
            </ul>

            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px] leading-relaxed">
              Travelers who explore additional areas around Ben Duoc can spend considerably longer than the standard 2–3-hour tunnel visit. Additional activities or special programmes should be confirmed separately from the standard 135,000 VND international charge.
            </p>
          </section>

          <!-- Section: Opening Hours & When To Visit -->
          <section id="opening-hours" class="scroll-mt-28 space-y-5">
            <div class="border-l-4 border-amber-500 pl-4 mb-4">
              <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl lg:text-[26px] font-bold text-slate-900 leading-tight">
                Opening Hours & When To Visit
              </h2>
            </div>

            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px] leading-relaxed">
              The site's published opening hours are:
            </p>

            <div class="p-6 bg-slate-900 text-white rounded-2xl text-center shadow-xs border border-slate-800 my-4">
              <div class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-amber-400 font-bold mb-1">Official Daily Operating Schedule</div>
              <div class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-serif font-bold text-white">7:00 AM – 5:00 PM daily</div>
              <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-400 mt-2">The site operates all days of the week</div>
            </div>

            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px] leading-relaxed">
              Morning is generally the most practical time to visit, especially if you want cooler conditions and more time for the rest of your day. The site opens at 7:00 AM, giving early visitors an opportunity to explore before the hottest part of the day.
            </p>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px] leading-relaxed">
              Early morning is generally quieter than later morning, when larger coach groups arrive. A departure around 7:00–8:00 AM is a practical planning window for travelers who want to avoid the hottest part of the day and return to Ho Chi Minh City around midday or early afternoon.
            </p>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px] leading-relaxed">
              If you plan to visit in the afternoon, leave enough buffer for traffic and the time needed to complete the site visit before closing. For detailed timing strategies, see: <a href="/how-to-visit-cu-chi-tunnels-without-the-crowds" class="text-amber-800 hover:underline font-semibold">How to Visit Cu Chi Tunnels Without the Crowds</a>.
            </p>
          </section>

          <!-- Section: How long do you need -->
          <section id="visit-duration" class="scroll-mt-28 space-y-5">
            <div class="border-l-4 border-amber-500 pl-4 mb-4">
              <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl lg:text-[26px] font-bold text-slate-900 leading-tight">
                How Long Do You Need At Cu Chi Tunnels?
              </h2>
            </div>

            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px] leading-relaxed">
              Most travelers should allow around <strong>2 to 3 hours on site</strong>, depending on the visitor route, guiding, tunnel crawl, and optional activities.
            </p>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px] leading-relaxed">
              Your actual visit can be shorter or longer depending on which site you visit, your guide, crowd levels, whether you enter the tunnel, how much time you spend photographing the site, whether you explore additional areas, and whether you participate in optional activities.
            </p>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px] leading-relaxed">
              Around <strong>5 to 6 hours is a useful planning range</strong> for a private half-day excursion door-to-door, but actual travel time varies with traffic and hotel location. Trying to fit Cu Chi into a very tight three-hour door-to-door schedule leaves little room for traffic or delays. For road logistics, see our guide on <a href="/how-far-are-the-cu-chi-tunnels-from-ho-chi-minh-city" class="text-amber-800 hover:underline font-semibold">How Far Are The Cu Chi Tunnels From Ho Chi Minh City?</a>.
            </p>
          </section>

          <!-- Section: Walking Distance -->
          <section id="walking-distance" class="scroll-mt-28 space-y-5">
            <div class="border-l-4 border-amber-500 pl-4 mb-4">
              <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl lg:text-[26px] font-bold text-slate-900 leading-tight">
                How Much Walking Is There At Cu Chi Tunnels?
              </h2>
            </div>

            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px] leading-relaxed">
              The standard visitor route involves roughly <strong>600 to 700 metres of walking</strong> in total, according to current visitor information, generally at an easy pace with stops along the way.
            </p>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px] leading-relaxed">
              The walking paths are flat and shaded under the forest canopy. Because the group pauses frequently to view concealed trapdoors, bomb craters, and historical exhibits, the physical exertion is modest for travelers with average mobility. The tunnel crawl itself is only one optional segment of this circuit.
            </p>
          </section>

          <!-- Section: Do you have to crawl -->
          <section id="tunnel-crawl-realities" class="scroll-mt-28 space-y-5">
            <div class="border-l-4 border-amber-500 pl-4 mb-4">
              <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl lg:text-[26px] font-bold text-slate-900 leading-tight">
                Do You Have To Crawl Through The Tunnels?
              </h2>
            </div>

            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px] leading-relaxed">
              <strong>No. The tunnel crawl is optional.</strong>
            </p>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px] leading-relaxed">
              Most of the visitor experience takes place above ground, including the forest paths, trapdoors, defensive displays and historical structures. The tunnel crawl is only one part of the visit.
            </p>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px] leading-relaxed">
              Visitor tunnel sections are available in different lengths, with travelers commonly reporting options around 20, 40, 60 and up to 100 metres. The underground experience is optional, and visitors can choose to stop early rather than completing the longest section. You do not need to force yourself through the tunnel to have a worthwhile visit.
            </p>

